WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.268
♪ (SOFT MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:08.274 line:0%
Hi, my name is Carrie Coon,

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.943
and welcome
to the latest edition

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:11.610
of Mansion Row on 5th Avenue.

00:00:11.677 --> 00:00:12.879
♪ (MUSIC BUILDS) ♪

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:17.083
<i> Bertha Russell's family,
 newly minted by
 the Transcontinental Railroad,</i>

00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:19.519
have arrived, and she wants
everyone to know it.

00:00:19.852 --> 00:00:21.621
And so, she's built
this palatial estate,

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:25.358
in the hopes that
she'll attract the curiosity
of fancy New York society.

00:00:25.391 --> 00:00:26.893
<i> These fixtures,</i>

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:28.795
<i> the gilded walls,
 the grand scale,</i>

00:00:28.861 --> 00:00:30.863
<i> it's all designed
 to signal to them that</i>

00:00:30.896 --> 00:00:32.098
she will not be ignored.

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:34.133
GEORGE RUSSELL:
They don't know we exist.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.235
Well they will now.

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:37.570
CARRIE COON:<i> Most of
 the fixtures you see here</i>

00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:39.305
were inspired by,
or copied from,

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:41.808
the historic mansions
in Newport, Rhode Island.

00:00:42.041 --> 00:00:43.242
This fireplace, for example,

00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:44.944
is modeled after those
in The Breakers.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.316
And of course, there's nothing
like a sweeping staircase

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:52.118
to put me right
in Bertha's shoes.

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:56.222
<i> And what better showcase
 is there for the beaded cape
 of a long train?</i>

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:58.558
Please,
come into the drawing room.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.062
COON:<i> The drawing room is one
of my favorite rooms on the set.</i>

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:06.833
And not just because
of the Boldini-inspired portrait
of Bertha on the wall.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.002
<i> Portraits by Boldini were
 very coveted status symbols,</i>

00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:12.171
<i> and Bertha has hers
 prominently displayed.</i>

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:14.274
Most of the furniture
you see in this room

00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:15.942
are antiques that
have been reupholstered.

00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:19.012
<i> Mirroring the cold limestone
 of the exterior,</i>

00:01:19.078 --> 00:01:23.949
Bertha's interior also features
more limestone, marble,
ample gold leaf,

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:26.952
<i> and bright colors designed to
emphasize the scale of the room.</i>

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:28.420
<i> These high ceilings
 could swallow</i>

00:01:28.454 --> 00:01:30.322
<i>the most intimidating adversary.</i>

00:01:30.589 --> 00:01:33.792
My goodness... How splendid.

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:36.162
♪ (DRAMATIC MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:40.199
The columns you see here
in the dining room

00:01:40.265 --> 00:01:42.535
were also fashioned
after those in The Breakers.

00:01:45.104 --> 00:01:47.841
This tablecloth was fabricated
by a seamstress in Russia,

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.542
<i> and based off of
 our team's research.</i>

00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:54.947
<i> Every detail on the set
 was meticulously researched
 and sourced</i>

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.849
by Regina Graves and her team.

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:58.785
This red damask, for example,

00:01:58.818 --> 00:02:01.353
was reprinted
by the original manufacturer

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:02.388
<i> from an old file.</i>

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:05.791
And, like her historical
counterpart, Alva Vanderbilt,

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:06.826
<i> Bertha would've been involved</i>

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:08.460
<i> at every level
 of decision-making.</i>

00:02:08.827 --> 00:02:10.396
We finished the gilding
in the ballroom, Mrs. Russell.

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:12.832
No, you think you have finished
the gilding, Mr. Kowalski,

00:02:12.865 --> 00:02:14.566
but nothing is finished
'til I decide.

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:16.669
COON:<i> Our production designer,
 Bob Shaw,</i>

00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:18.204
<i> says that Bertha's bedroom</i>

00:02:18.237 --> 00:02:20.139
is a tribute
to Alva Vanderbilt's bedroom

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:21.674
at the Marble House
in Newport.

00:02:21.707 --> 00:02:24.143
<i> It's filled
 with antique trinkets,</i>

00:02:24.176 --> 00:02:25.311
<i> historical reproductions.</i>

00:02:25.878 --> 00:02:27.346
<i> If someone were to wander in
 from the Brook House,</i>

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.248
they might ask,
"Where's the sink?"

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:30.349
<i> Betraying, once again,</i>

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:32.017
<i> the Russell's
 extravagant wealth.</i>

00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:34.386
Whereas the Brook House would've
had sinks in the bedroom,

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.255
and a shared common toilet,

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:39.092
the Russell's have
their toilet and bathtub
in one modern bathroom.

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:40.526
I know, it's shocking.

00:02:40.893 --> 00:02:43.195
Alleluia.
Let the trumpets sound.

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:46.231
Thank you for joining me
on this tour of Bertha's
extraordinary home.

00:02:46.265 --> 00:02:47.734
♪ (MUSIC SWELLS) ♪

00:02:50.836 --> 00:02:52.572 line:0%
♪ (MUSIC SOFTENS) ♪

